{"kind":"report","text":"Approved exacta99155faccd04f1390b2eec87aaa57bf4f1e58f8 as a design note only. Sole primary52665cc8dc3edbfa06a3debd5e6948c6405f7908 read first/full; all422lines reviewed, including both findings maps and the83-addition/24-deletion successor diff. No remaining blocking finding.\n\nArchitecture: the remaining hold finding is closed at design level. The hold key includes check/source/stage/configuration/rules generation/extent; recovery must be a current-rules admitted job bound to that exact key and a same-or-later publication. Policy changes preserve old holds until an explicit transition or override. Publication and within-publication job order are the destination's recorded orders, not hashes, unrelated IDs or notice arrival. A running published check does not block admission; failure plus hold is one destination update serialized with admissions, without retroactive effect on already admitted publications. The witnesses now distinguish that race and wrong-generation recovery. This is a coherent stated first-slice policy, not a current-health guarantee or a pending-check gate.\n\nSecurity: every:1 remains the only first-slice audit setting; independent sampling/anti-grinding is explicitly future. Audit targets must execute fresh, rules reject reuse:true for them, and the destination cannot substitute an old pass after cancellation/retry. That closes the audit/reuse finding. Configuration/stage/subject comparability, authoritative job/source admission and unknown/timeout not being a pass remain explicit. Holds cannot be cleared by another check/configuration/generation, closing an intent, or a disguised healthy outcome; transitions and overrides remain separately attributed authority.\n\nSimplification: retain source/reuse/stage, existing member defaults and versioned adoption. The index avoids whole-room history search, compares admitted pass/fail outcomes under one eligibility boundary, and disables reuse without blocking a fresh fenced run